Dear Laura,

Solidon3D 0.3.1 has been released. The relevant TCT angle is what happens to
data provenance when imported geometry is edited: a mesh carries no design
history, yet downstream steps need something stable to refer to.

Solidon recognises features in imported STL or 3MF — holes, pegs,
countersinks, domes — and this release lets them be moved, rotated, resized,
duplicated and removed while the original axis and, importantly, the feature
identity are preserved. The common workaround of filling a feature and
recreating it produces equivalent geometry but a new identity, orphaning every
fit or annotation that referenced it. Keeping the identity is what makes the
edit safe for a parameterised workflow.

Around that, the release adds a feature panel presenting measured values as
editable fields with a live preview, a single-transaction edit across all
features of the same kind, and a manufacturability bound on recognition itself:
features below half a millimetre are no longer reported, which removed 296 of
1130 entries on a sample part.

For the handoff, each physical filament spool still carries temperatures,
cooling, retraction and material properties into the correct 3MF material
position; the printer profiles offered are now filtered to the configured
machine rather than the slicer's entire inventory.

This is desktop polymer-AM preparation rather than enterprise PLM or a slicer.
Every change is a reproducible operation; an optional language model may call
the same fixed API as the UI, but cannot calculate geometry or execute
generated code.

Solidon runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an account, telemetry or cloud
project store: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can provide a compact technical briefing, the released 0.3.1 build, a sample
STL-to-3MF workflow and publication-ready imagery.

